# shipped_build.defs -- the compile-time default macros the SHIPPED load-slot dll was built with.
# One /D argument per line (this file is fed to tools/build_shipped.ps1, which prefixes each
# non-comment line with /D). Values are the game-slot truth, recorded 2026-11-03.
#
# Provenance (do not edit without re-proving the reproduction):
#   out\DEPLOY.md5.txt 2026-11-02 promote2 block, lines 49-52:
#     new dll md5 = ABB969FE4AAF0F94AA03C3F53781EDD0  (cand_skip, 3,813,376 B)
#     source      = worktree wt-08e8b62 (commit 08e8b62 + tools/patch_testdefs.py hook applied)
#     flags       = /DT_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF="18,19,20,21,22,23,24,26,27,28,29,30,31,32", no DM_BAR
#   Evidence printed by that dll: chains_skipped=14 refused=0 closures_noop=168 passes=691
#
# NOTE the macro NAMES are T_*_DEF; the build line reads //D + T_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF under MSYS
# (i.e. /DT_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF). Grepping the tree for "DT_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF" finds nothing -- that
# was the 2026-11-03 confusion; grep for T_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF instead.
#
# QUOTING: this file is consumed by tools/build_shipped.ps1 (Windows PowerShell 5.1 -> native
# icx.exe). Plain double quotes are STRIPPED on the way to the compiler, which turned the macro
# into the integer 18 and broke two hook sites (1108 / 1180, 2026-11-03 11:4x). The escaped form
# below is what survives: the compiler finally sees a string literal. If you ever drive this from
# MSYS bash instead, write it as //DT_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF="18,...".
T_SKIP_CHAINS_DEF=\"18,19,20,21,22,23,24,26,27,28,29,30,31,32\"
